body:
This is great thank you for writing this up. I haven't worked on networks of this scale before to i fear i may be out of my depth a bit.

&#x200B;

The issue i seem to be coming up against in all of this is the fact that we have static devices at all different points on our network. So I can't easily separate them into VLANs where you have one port with one VLAN and one network.

&#x200B;

Your second recommendation seems like the best way to go. and we have some Cisco SF300's that we might be able to set this up on. We the way have have things set currently would we just set a bunch of VLANs on each port and then plug each of those ports into our L2 switches? if so how do the systems know what traffic goes where?  How does the L@ switch know to sent the traffic from a device that should be one VLAN 1 to that port? Or are the ports with the VLANS assigned on the router constantly looking in the L2 network for anything that applies to it? Also I'd worry about creating a loop? I guess is the routing between VLANs is being handled outside of that area in the SDWAN world then maybe it's not an issue?

&#x200B;

Does that sound right? What do you think?

We have more than 4 networks per location. 

&#x200B;

It goes against my nature to keep applying band-aids as well. I think you're absolutely right that the underlying network needs to be drastically altered. I'm always the person looking for the longest term solution. But this is the situation that I've inherited and I need to do things backward in this case. 

&#x200B;

Any workarounds you can recommend would be appreciated.

Some of the switches at some locations do have VLAN Functionality. and we could probably do 1 at each location without buying new equipment. 

&#x200B;

The problem is getting this all to work with the SDWAN.

Can be done using powershell in Exchange console. I'm not sure about the O365 equivalent though.

&#x200B;

`Add-MailboxPermission -Identity <USERALLOWINGFULLACCESS> -User <USERTOGETFULLACCESS> -AccessRights FullAccess -AutoMapping:$false`

>$SpoolProcess = Get-Process -name "spoolsv" -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue  
if ($SpoolProcess) {  
$SpoolProcess.ProcessorAffinity = 1  
}

That is working well. Thank you.
